<h2 id="install">Install</h2>
<p>
    DbnClient is published on NuGet as <a href="@Links.NuGetClient">DbnClient</a>. It targets .NET 11 and is Native AOT
    compatible, and its only dependencies are the AWS S3 SDK, ZstdSharp and System.IO.Hashing. Tapes, in
    <code>DbnClient.Tape</code>, need version 0.2.0 or later.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="In your project's folder" Text="dotnet add package DbnClient" />
<p>Run the same command again later to move to the newest version.</p>

<h2 id="open">Open an archive</h2>
<p>
    <code>DbnArchive.Open</code> takes the archive's S3 address and a read-only key, or a folder. Files from S3 are
    kept in the cache folder you give, so reading a day twice downloads it once. A folder archive needs no cache.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="C#" Text="@Open" />
<p>
    <code>ArchiveOptions</code> also takes <code>Bucket</code> (<code>dbn</code>), <code>Prefix</code>,
    <code>Region</code> and <code>Dataset</code> (<code>GLBX.MDP3</code>). Never put a secret in source: read it from
    configuration, user secrets or the environment.
</p>

<h2 id="list">List what it holds</h2>
<CodeBlock Title="C#" Text="@List" />
<p><code>ListDaysAsync(schema, root, cancel)</code> lists one series' days, each with its stored object; <code>Empty</code> marks a day with no data.</p>

<h2 id="read">Read records</h2>
<p>
    <code>OpenAsync</code> fetches one UTC day (through the cache) and returns a <code>DbnReader</code>, which streams
    the day's records as typed C# records. Prices are <code>decimal?</code>, null when Databento® has none; times are
    nanoseconds since the UNIX epoch, UTC.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="C#: every trade of a day" Text="@Read" />
<div class="table">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Schemas</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>MboRecord</code></td><td><code>mbo</code>: an order added, modified, cancelled, filled or traded. <code>Action</code> is <code>A</code>, <code>M</code>, <code>C</code>, <code>F</code>, <code>T</code> or <code>R</code> (clear).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>OhlcvRecord</code></td><td><code>ohlcv-1s</code>, <code>-1m</code>, <code>-1h</code>, <code>-1d</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>DefinitionRecord</code></td><td><code>definition</code>: symbol, expiry, tick size and the rest.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>StatRecord</code></td><td><code>statistics</code>: settlement, open interest, session high and low, and more.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>StatusRecord</code></td><td><code>status</code>: trading halts, pre-open, open and close.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>OtherRecord</code></td><td>Anything else, by type and length.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>
    <code>reader.Metadata</code> holds the file's DBN metadata, including the symbol mappings. To name instruments,
    <code>archive.InstrumentsAsync(root, day, cancel)</code> maps each instrument id to its <code>Contract</code>.
    <code>DbnTime.ToTime</code> converts a time to <code>DateTimeOffset</code>, and <code>Zones.Format</code> writes
    one in any zone.
</p>
<p>
    <code>DbnReader.OpenAsync(stream, cancel)</code> reads any zstd-compressed DBN version 3 stream, so it works on
    files from Databento itself too.
</p>

<h2 id="export">Export</h2>
<p>
    <code>Exporter</code> does everything <a href="docs/dbnfetch#export"><code>dbnf export</code></a> does. Build an
    <code>ExportRequest</code>; the parsers return results, so check each or, as here for brevity, take
    <code>.Value</code> of inputs you know are good.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="C#: 5-minute RTH bars of back-adjusted ES, as one Parquet file" Text="@Export" />
<div class="table">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>ExportRequest</th><th>Default</th><th>dbnf option</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>Format</code></td><td><code>Csv</code></td><td><code>--format</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Compression</code></td><td><code>None</code></td><td><code>--compress</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Layout</code></td><td><code>PerDay</code></td><td><code>--single</code>, <code>--partition</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>NameTemplate</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>--name</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Zone</code></td><td>UTC</td><td><code>--tz</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Time</code>, <code>SplitTime</code></td><td><code>Iso</code>, false</td><td><code>--time</code>, <code>--split-time</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Prices</code></td><td><code>Decimal</code></td><td><code>--price</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Columns</code></td><td>all</td><td><code>--columns</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Bars</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>--bars</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>As</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>--as</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Adjustment</code>, <code>RollDays</code></td><td><code>None</code>, the root's <code>CalendarRule</code></td><td><code>--adjust</code>, <code>--roll-days</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Spreads</code></td><td>true</td><td><code>--no-spreads</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>Note that <code>Zone</code> defaults to UTC in the library, where <code>dbnf</code> defaults to Chicago.</p>

<h2 id="rolls">Rolls and contracts</h2>
<CodeBlock Title="C#" Text="@Rolls" />
<p>
    <code>roll.TradeDate</code> is the first trade date the new contract is used, so a roll decided over a weekend is
    dated Monday. <code>schedule.On(tradeDate)</code> says which contract the series holds on a trade date.
    <code>schedule.Adjuster(tradeDate, adjustment)</code> gives what a price of that date becomes, as a result: it is
    <code>Errors.NoSettlement</code> if a later roll has no gap or ratio to adjust by.
    <code>archive.ContractsAsync(root, progress, cancel)</code> lists every contract of a root from its definitions.
    <code>Sessions.TradeDate(nanos)</code> gives the CME trade date of a time, and <code>Sessions.RegularHours(root)</code>
    a root's regular hours.
</p>

<h2 id="tapes">Tapes</h2>
<p>
    <code>DbnClient.Tape</code> builds and replays compact files for backtesting: <code>Catalog</code> and
    <code>TapeBuilder</code> make them from an archive, and <code>Tape.LoadAsync</code> with a cursor replays them with
    nothing allocated per event. See <a href="docs/dbntape">DbnTape</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="errors">Errors</h2>
<p>
    Expected failures come back as a <code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code> carrying an <code>Error</code> with a stable code,
    rather than as exceptions. Check <code>IsSuccess</code> before reading <code>Value</code>. A damaged file is a result
    from <code>OpenAsync</code>, <code>InstrumentsAsync</code>, <code>ContractsAsync</code>, <code>RollSchedule.BuildAsync</code>
    and <code>Exporter.RunAsync</code>. Only the reader itself throws: if the damage is further in than
    <code>OpenAsync</code> looked, <code>reader.ReadAsync</code> throws <code>ArchiveException</code>, whose
    <code>Error</code> is the same one. A <code>DbnReader</code> you open on a stream of your own throws
    <code>InvalidDataException</code>.
</p>
<CodeBlock Title="C#" Text="@Errors" />
<div class="table">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Errors.</th><th>Code</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>NotStored</code></td><td><code>archive.not_stored</code></td><td>The archive has no file for that day.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>NoData</code></td><td><code>archive.no_data</code></td><td>The day was fetched and had no data.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Denied</code></td><td><code>archive.denied</code></td><td>The key was refused, or doesn't reach the bucket.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Unreachable</code></td><td><code>archive.unreachable</code></td><td>The archive couldn't be reached.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>UnknownLayout</code></td><td><code>archive.unknown_layout</code></td><td>The files are in a layout this version doesn't know.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Corrupt</code></td><td><code>archive.corrupt</code></td><td>A day's file is damaged. The message names it and says what to do.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>NoSettlement</code></td><td><code>export.no_settlement</code></td><td>An adjusted export crosses a roll with no gap or ratio. The message names the roll and its contracts.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>BadSymbol</code></td><td><code>input.bad_symbol</code></td><td>Not a root, a contract or a continuous name.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

